\section{Which margin is clustered}{
\code{cluster_scale = "samples"}, the default, puts one point per row of \code{data}, and
the question is which samples resemble each other. \code{"features"} puts one point
per column, and the question is which features move together. The second is the
one this and \code{\link[=draw_heatmap]{draw_heatmap()}} agree on: the features are standardised first and
the transpose is clustered as it stands, which is also what
\code{perform_pca(embedding_scale = "features")} reports on.

Transposing \code{data} by hand instead is a third analysis. \code{\link[=scale]{scale()}} standardises
\strong{columns}, so on the transpose it standardises samples, and the picture looks
right while answering a different question. \code{cluster_scale} exists so that the
transpose never has to be taken by hand.
}

\section{Choosing the distance and the linkage}{
\code{dist_method} decides what "close" means and \code{hclust_method} decides what
"close" means for two groups rather than two points. \code{"correlation"} is
\code{1 - cor()}, so two points a constant apart are at distance zero: use it when
the shape of a profile is the question and its level is not. The default
\code{"average"} linkage is the one that neither chains clusters into strings the way
single linkage does nor insists they come out round the way \code{"ward.D2"} does.

The tree is built on exactly the distance \code{\link[=draw_heatmap]{draw_heatmap()}} builds its
dendrogram from, so a heatmap drawn with the same \code{dist_method} and
\code{hclust_method} is showing this clustering and not a near relative of it.
}

\section{What is dropped before anything runs}{
Rows that are not complete and finite across \code{feats} go before the distance is
measured, and \code{design$n_dropped} reports how many. This is the listwise deletion
the rest of the package uses; nothing is imputed. A feature that takes a single
value cannot be scaled, so with \code{scale = TRUE} it is left out with a message and
named in \code{design$dropped_feats}.
}

